/* Logic: 
1. Recursion (Memoization)

- Start from the last indices of both strings: `n1-1` and `n2-1`.
- If either index becomes negative, return `0` because no common subsequence is possible.
- If the result is already calculated, return the stored value from `dp`.
- If the current characters match, add `1` and move both indices backward by one.
- If the characters do not match, try two possibilities: move the first index backward or move the second index backward. Take the maximum of both results.
- Store the result in `dp[idx1][idx2]` to avoid repeated calculations.
- Return the answer for the last indices of both strings.

Time Complexity: O(n1 × n2)

Space Complexity: O(n1 × n2) for DP, plus O(n1 + n2) for the recursion stack.

 2. Tabulation (Bottom-Up DP)

- Create a 2D DP array of size `(n1+1) × (n2+1)`, initialized with zeros.
- The first row and first column represent an empty string, so their LCS length is `0`.
- Traverse both strings using indices from `1` to their respective lengths.
- If the current characters match, set `dp[i][j] = 1 + dp[i-1][j-1]`.
- If the characters do not match, set `dp[i][j] = max(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1])`.
- Continue filling the table until all subproblems are solved.
- Return `dp[n1][n2]`, which contains the LCS length of both complete strings.

Time Complexity: O(n1 × n2)

Space Complexity: O(n1 × n2)

 3. Space Optimization (Two Rows)

- Instead of storing the entire 2D DP table, maintain two 1D arrays: `prev` and `curr`, each of size `n2+1`.
- Initialize both arrays with zeros to handle empty prefixes.
- Traverse the first string using the outer loop and the second string using the inner loop.
- If the current characters match, set `curr[j] = 1 + prev[j-1]`.
- If they do not match, set `curr[j] = max(prev[j], curr[j-1])`.
- After completing each row, copy `curr` into `prev` so the next iteration can use the previous row's results.
- Return `prev[n2]` after processing both strings.

Time Complexity: O(n1 × n2)

Space Complexity: O(n2) for the two arrays.

Note: To achieve O(min(n1, n2)) space, make the shorter string the second string before creating the arrays.
*/

class Solution {
private:
    int solve(int idx1, int idx2, string& t1, string& t2,
              vector<vector<int>>& dp) {

        // Base case: if either string is exhausted,
        // no common subsequence is possible
        if (idx1 < 0 || idx2 < 0)
            return 0;

        // If the result is already calculated, return it
        if (dp[idx1][idx2] != -1)
            return dp[idx1][idx2];

        // If current characters match, include this character
        // and move diagonally to the previous characters
        if (t1[idx1] == t2[idx2]) {
            return dp[idx1][idx2] = 1 + solve(idx1 - 1, idx2 - 1, t1, t2, dp);
        }

        // If characters don't match, try both possibilities:
        // 1. Skip the current character from t1
        // 2. Skip the current character from t2
        // Take the maximum of both results
        return dp[idx1][idx2] = max(solve(idx1 - 1, idx2, t1, t2, dp),
                                    solve(idx1, idx2 - 1, t1, t2, dp));
    }

public:
    int longestCommonSubsequence(string text1, string text2) {
        int n1 = text1.size();
        int n2 = text2.size();

        // Initialize DP table with -1 to indicate
        // that no subproblem has been solved yet
        vector<vector<int>> dp(n1, vector<int>(n2, -1));

        // Start recursion from the last indices
        // of both strings
        return solve(n1 - 1, n2 - 1, text1, text2, dp);
    }
};
